### I CANNOT SOLVE THIS

Ringel's conjecture (1963) is a famous **open problem** in graph theory. The problem statement itself explicitly acknowledges this: *"It remains open; the case of all sufficiently large $n$ is proved by Montgomery–Pokrovskiy–Sudakov."* The Lean theorem is annotated `@[category research open, AMS 5]`, marking it as an open research conjecture.

The conjecture asserts that for any tree $T$ with $n$ edges, the complete graph $K_{2n+1}$ decomposes into $2n+1$ edge-disjoint copies of $T$. Only the asymptotic case (all sufficiently large $n$) has been resolved, by Montgomery, Pokrovskiy, and Sudakov (2020). The full conjecture — in particular, the small-$n$ cases and a complete proof for all $n$ — remains unsolved, and no proof is known to the mathematical community.

Since this is an open research problem with no known complete proof, I cannot provide one.
